Closure:
A closure is a function along with a reference to its lexical environment.
When an inner function is returned and assigned to a variable, that variable holds the function, 
and the function retains access to the lexical variables of its parent scope,
even after the parent function has finished execution.

function outer() {
  let x = 10;
  return function inner() {
    console.log(x);
  };
}

const fn = outer();
fn();
What actually happens:
sql
Copy code
fn ──▶ inner function
          │
          ▼
   [[Environment]] ──▶ Outer Lexical Environment (x = 10)
✔ fn does NOT store the environment
✔ inner function stores it internally ([[Environment]])

A closure keeps variables alive by retaining a reference to its lexical environment.
Memory is released when the function becomes unreachable.
Improper use of closures—especially with event listeners or subscriptions—can cause memory leaks.

