===========================================Hot code=========================================================================
How V8 detects hot code

While Ignition (interpreter) executes bytecode, it records feedback:

Function call count

Loop back-edge count

Type stability (number + number)

Object shape consistency

When thresholds are crossed → hot

✅ What counts as hot code (with examples)
1️⃣ Repeated function calls ✅ (HOT)
function sum(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

for (let i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
  sum(1, 2);
}


✔ sum() becomes hot
✔ TurboFan optimizes it

2️⃣ Loops (VERY HOT) ✅
for (let i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
  total += i;
}


✔ Loop back-edge executed many times
✔ JIT aggressively optimizes loops

3️⃣ Frequently executed callbacks ✅
setInterval(() => {
  processData();
}, 10);


✔ Callback runs repeatedly
✔ processData() becomes hot

❗ Not because it’s a callback
✔ Because it runs often

4️⃣ Event handlers that fire often ✅
window.addEventListener('mousemove', handler);


✔ handler becomes hot
✔ Optimized for speed

5️⃣ Promise .then() handlers ⚠️ (sometimes hot)
fetchData().then(process);


Single execution → ❌ not hot

Inside loop / repeated API → ✅ hot

❌ What is NOT hot code
1️⃣ Code executed once ❌
initApp();


✔ No repetition → no optimization

2️⃣ Rare error paths ❌
if (error) handleError();


✔ Cold path → not optimized

3️⃣ Unstable code ❌
function add(x, y) {
  return x + y;
}

add(1, 2);      // number
add(1, "2");    // string


❌ Types change
❌ De-optimized repeatedly
❌ Avoided by JIT

